<section class="critique-section">
    <div class="critique-header">
        <h2>Interactive Peer Review</h2>
        <p>Proactive responses to common econometric and historical critiques. Click a question to see the scholarly justification.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="critique-grid">
        <details class="critique-card">
            <summary>
                <span class="critique-tag">Econometrics</span>
                <h3>"How do you account for Endogenous Sorting?"</h3>
                <span class="plus">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="critique-answer">
                <p>The concern is that 'better' parents might have moved into textile districts before the Act. However, 19th-century migration was primarily driven by labor demand, not school preferences. I address this using <strong>Lagged Textile Intensity</strong> and showing that fertility trends were parallel for 20 years <em>prior</em> to the 1833 shock.</p>
            </div>
        </details>

        <details class="critique-card">
            <summary>
                <span class="critique-tag">History</span>
                <h3>"Wasn't fertility already declining in some regions?"</h3>
                <span class="plus">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="critique-answer">
                <p>While some urban centers saw early declines, our DiD framework uses <strong>District Fixed Effects</strong> to net out these baseline differences. We isolate the <em>acceleration</em> of the decline that specifically coincides with the inspectorate's arrival in textile parishes.</p>
            </div>
        </details>

        <details class="critique-card">
            <summary>
                <span class="critique-tag">Data</span>
                <h3>"How reliable are the OCR transcriptions?"</h3>
                <span class="plus">+</span>
            </summary>
            <div class="critique-answer">
                <p>We ran a 5% manual validation check on the 1851 census transcriptions. The error rate was <0.8%. Furthermore, since errors are likely <em>classical</em> (non-systematic), they would bias our results toward zero, making our significant findings even more robust.</p>
            </div>
        </details>
    </div>
</section>

<style>
    .critique-section {
        margin: 6rem 0;
        padding: 4rem;
        background: rgba(255,255,255,0.02);
        border-radius: 16px;
        border: 1px solid rgba(212, 175, 55, 0.1);
    }
    .critique-header { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 4rem; }
    .critique-header h2 { font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
    .critique-header p { opacity: 0.6; max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto; }

    .critique-grid {
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 1.5rem;
    }

    .critique-card {
        background: var(--secondary);
        border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);
        border-radius: 8px;
        overflow: hidden;
        transition: all 0.3s;
    }
    .critique-card[open] {
        border-color: var(--accent);
    }

    summary {
        padding: 2rem;
        list-style: none;
        cursor: pointer;
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        position: relative;
    }
    summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }

    .critique-tag {
        position: absolute;
        top: 0.8rem;
        left: 2rem;
        font-size: 0.6rem;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--accent);
        letter-spacing: 0.1rem;
        opacity: 0.8;
    }

    .critique-card h3 {
        margin: 0;
        font-size: 1.2rem;
        font-family: var(--sans);
        color: var(--text);
    }

    .plus {
        font-size: 1.5rem;
        color: var(--accent);
        transition: transform 0.3s;
    }
    .critique-card[open] .plus {
        transform: rotate(45deg);
    }

    .critique-answer {
        padding: 0 2rem 2rem 2rem;
        font-size: 1rem;
        line-height: 1.6;
        opacity: 0.9;
        border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.05);
        padding-top: 1.5rem;
    }
    .critique-answer strong { color: var(--accent); }
</style>
